  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>To be your own general contractor, you take over the job a builder normally does: hiring and scheduling every trade, buying materials, pulling permits, managing the budget, and making sure the work passes inspection. It can save real money and give you control over every detail, but only if you treat it like a job, not a hobby. Here is what the role actually involves and how to set yourself up to succeed.</p>

<h2>What a general contractor actually does</h2>
<p>Most people picture a GC swinging a hammer. In reality, a good GC is a project manager. The work breaks down into a few core responsibilities:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Estimating and budgeting:</strong> turning plans into a line-item budget and keeping it current.</li>
<li><strong>Hiring and contracting:</strong> getting bids, checking licenses and insurance, and signing clear scopes of work.</li>
<li><strong>Scheduling:</strong> sequencing trades so the plumber is not waiting on the framer, and materials arrive just in time.</li>
<li><strong>Supervising quality:</strong> walking the site, catching mistakes before they are covered up.</li>
<li><strong>Managing inspections:</strong> calling inspections, meeting inspectors and fixing corrections.</li>
<li><strong>Paying and documenting:</strong> paying subs against completed work and collecting lien waivers.</li>
<li><strong>Problem solving:</strong> weather, back-ordered windows, a sub who stops answering the phone.</li>
</ul>
<p>If that list sounds energizing, owner-building may be a fit. If it sounds exhausting, there is no shame in hiring a builder.</p>

<h2>Be honest about your situation</h2>
<p>Before you commit, check yourself against these realities:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Time:</strong> You will take calls during the workday and visit the site often, sometimes daily.</li>
<li><strong>Proximity:</strong> Running a build from two hours away is much harder than from twenty minutes.</li>
<li><strong>Temperament:</strong> You need to be organized, decisive and comfortable holding people accountable.</li>
<li><strong>Knowledge:</strong> You do not need to be a tradesperson, but you need to understand the sequence of construction and what good work looks like.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Financing and insurance hurdles</h2>
<h3>Construction loans</h3>
<p>Lenders worry about owner-builders because a stalled project is a bad loan. Some banks will not lend to owner-builders at all; others will with conditions. Expect to provide a detailed budget, construction schedule, full plans, signed sub bids, and proof of relevant experience or training. A larger down payment or land equity helps. Talk to local banks and credit unions early. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-finance-building-a-house/">how to finance building a house</a> covers the options.</p>
<h3>Insurance</h3>
<p>You need coverage before the first load of material arrives. Talk with an insurance agent about:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Builder's risk:</strong> covers the structure and materials during construction against fire, wind and theft, depending on the policy.</li>
<li><strong>Liability:</strong> protects you if someone is injured on your site.</li>
<li><strong>Sub coverage:</strong> require certificates of insurance from every subcontractor showing general liability and, ideally, workers' compensation.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Permits and codes</h2>
<p>Texas does not license residential general contractors at the state level, and many Texas cities allow homeowners to pull a building permit for a house they will live in. Electrical, plumbing and HVAC work generally must be done and permitted by state-licensed trades. Rules vary, so confirm with your city or county before you plan around anything. Start with our overview of <a href="/navigating-texas-building-codes-what-you-need-to-know/">Texas building codes</a> and the guide for your city.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Visit your permitting office in person before you submit anything. Ask what owner-builders most often get wrong. Ten minutes with a friendly plans examiner can save you a full review cycle.</p></div>

<h2>Picking and managing subcontractors</h2>
<p>Your subs make or break the job. Get at least three bids for each major trade, compare scopes line by line rather than just bottom-line price, and check references on recent jobs. Put everything in writing: scope, price, payment schedule, start date and cleanup expectations. Pay only for completed work and collect lien waivers with each payment. Our full guide to <a href="/how-to-hire-and-manage-subcontractors/">hiring and managing subcontractors</a> walks through each step.</p>

<h2>How much can you really save?</h2>
<p>General contractor fees and markups often land somewhere around 10 to 20 percent of construction cost, so that is the theoretical ceiling on savings. Realistically, owner-builders often keep less than that because:</p>
<ul>
<li>Some subs charge owner-builders more than they charge repeat builders.</li>
<li>You will not get builder volume pricing on materials.</li>
<li>Mistakes, rework and schedule gaps cost money.</li>
<li>Carrying costs (loan interest, rent) grow if the build runs long.</li>
</ul>
<p>A well-organized owner-builder can still save a meaningful amount and end up with a better-built house. Just plan with conservative numbers and a healthy contingency. Our <a href="/budgeting-for-your-texas-home-build-tips-for-staying-on-track/">budgeting guide</a> shows how.</p>

<h2>Learn the process before you start</h2>
<p>The single best investment you can make is education before you break ground. A few books are widely recommended for first-time owner-builders. [[amz:books_heldmann|Be Your Own House Contractor]] is a popular, practical walkthrough of the GC role, and the Complete Guide to Contracting Your Home goes deep on scheduling and budgeting.</p>
<!--products:books_heldmann,books_contracting_guide,books_dummies|Books for first-time owner-builders-->
<p>Pair your reading with a dedicated [[amz:home_planner|house-building planner]] to track bids, schedules and selections in one place.</p>

<h2>A simple owner-builder game plan</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Get educated first.</strong> Learn the construction sequence and the vocabulary before you talk to lenders or subs.</li>
<li><strong>Lock down plans and selections.</strong> Finished plans and a complete selections list make bids accurate and keep changes to a minimum.</li>
<li><strong>Build a line-item budget</strong> with a contingency, often somewhere around 10 percent or more for first-timers.</li>
<li><strong>Line up financing and insurance</strong> before you schedule the first trade.</li>
<li><strong>Collect bids and sign subs</strong> for the early phases first, then the later trades while the frame goes up.</li>
<li><strong>Pull permits</strong> and set up a job binder for every document.</li>
<li><strong>Run the job:</strong> daily check-ins, weekly schedule updates, and a walk before every inspection.</li>
</ol>
<p>Owner-builders who follow a plan like this tend to finish closer to budget than those who figure it out as they go. Keep a running list of decisions and questions so nothing waits on you.</p>

